# ---------- Deployment-only (documented here so they aren't forgotten) ----------
# Trusted reverse-proxy hops for reading the client IP from X-Forwarded-For.
# Read as chain[length - 1 - hops], so 0 = rightmost entry.
# The rightmost entry is always written by the last TRUSTED thing in front of the
# app, so 0 is correct on both platforms for different reasons:
#   Vercel -> its edge overwrites x-forwarded-for with the real client IP (it
#             refuses to forward client-supplied ones, to stop spoofing), so the
#             chain is length 1. No nginx involved; the edge IS the proxy.
#   EC2    -> nginx is the last hop, and whether it appends
#             ($proxy_add_x_forwarded_for) or replaces ($remote_addr), the
#             rightmost value is the address nginx actually saw.
#   1      -> ONLY if you add Cloudflare / an ALB IN FRONT OF nginx.
# Too HIGH is the dangerous direction: it reads attacker-supplied entries and the
# per-IP auth rate limit silently stops working. See apps/web/src/lib/env.ts.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Do NOT set NODE_ENV here — it would break local dev. Set it in the deploy
# environment instead (docker-compose.prod.yml; Vercel sets it automatically).
# NODE_ENV=production
#
# In production APP_URL must be the real https:// domain — it drives OAuth
# callbacks, canonical/OG URLs, the sitemap, the Secure flag on auth cookies, AND
# the origin of the /api/auth/refresh redirects.
#
# REDIS_URL is REQUIRED when NODE_ENV=production (env.ts throws without it).
# On EC2 the host is the compose service name `redis`, not localhost.

